From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kan Zhu <kanzhu@cs.washington.edu>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 06:47:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/18] feat(alignment): restore the bulk token and
 routed-expert dumps

The v0.23.0 rebase dropped `routing_trace.py` and its two dumps. Most of what
went with it was deliberate -- the CUDA-event forward/postprocess/sample timing
it also carried was replaced by the `vllm_iteration(N)` NVTX ranges plus nsys,
which measure the same spans without the synchronizes. These two dumps were not
replaced, so they come back.

`dump_token_inputs` writes the scheduled input token IDs, split back into
per-request spans. Nothing else recovers the exact token stream the engine saw;
a replay would otherwise have to re-tokenize prompt text and hope it matches.

`dump_routing_summary` writes per-layer routed expert histograms with the
physical/local split and the expert map. The `VibeSimAlignmentExpertLoad` record
overlaps it but is not a substitute: that one reads EPLB's accumulated load, so
it needs EPLB enabled and only fires on rearrangement steps, while this reads
the raw topk_ids capture for one nominated iteration.

Three things differ from the pre-rebase version:

* The file lands at `vllm/v1/worker/alignment_trace.py`, not under `fused_moe/`.
  Both dumps are called only from `gpu_model_runner`, and a token dump is not a
  MoE concern.
* Iteration indices are `scheduler_output.alignment_iteration_index` -- a real
  field now, not a `getattr` against a name that might not be there -- so a dump
  row joins to a `vllm_iteration(N)` NVTX range and to that iteration's
  `VibeSimAlignmentIteration` record with no timestamp matching.
* `RoutedExpertsCapturer` is upstream now, with a public `device_buffer` and its
  own `enable_return_routed_experts` gate, so the old `layer.py` capture hook is
  gone. Only the gate in `gpu_worker` is widened, so setting the routing-dump
  path is enough to bring the capturer up.

Env vars are renamed `VLLM_MOESIM_*` -> `VLLM_VIBESIM_*` to match the rest of
the fork; the old names are not honored.

The span split and the grouped-GEMM padding math are the two places this can be
wrong without failing, so both are pulled out as pure functions and unit tested
(12 cases, no GPU).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
